## 1. The Problem: Life Before Executor Framework

Before Java 5, if you wanted concurrent execution, you had to manually create, manage, and destroy threads. This led to serious problems.

### 1.1 Manual Thread Creation (The Old Way)

```java
// ❌ BAD PRACTICE — Creating a new thread for every task
public class OldThreadApproach {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {

        // For each task, a new Thread is created manually
        for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
            final int taskId = i;

            Thread thread = new Thread(new Runnable() {
                @Override
                public void run() {
                    System.out.println("Task " + taskId + " running on: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
                    // Simulate work
                    try { Thread.sleep(500); } catch (InterruptedException e) { e.printStackTrace(); }
                }
            });

            thread.start(); // Creates a brand-new OS thread every time!
        }

        // Problem: No way to know when all tasks are done without complex join() calls
    }
}
```

### 1.2 Problems with Manual Thread Management

| Problem | Description |
|---|---|
| **Thread creation overhead** | Creating an OS thread is expensive (~1MB stack, kernel involvement) |
| **No upper bound** | Unbounded threads exhaust memory/CPU |
| **No task queue** | No built-in mechanism to hold pending tasks |
| **No return values** | `Thread.run()` returns `void` — you can't get a result |
| **No exception propagation** | Exceptions in threads are silently swallowed unless you add handlers |
| **No lifecycle control** | No clean way to shut down a pool of threads |
| **No scheduling** | No easy way to run a task after a delay or periodically |

```java
// ❌ This kind of code was common before Java 5 — and it's dangerous!
public class ThreadPoolManual {

    private static final int POOL_SIZE = 5;
    private final Thread[] threads = new Thread[POOL_SIZE];
    private final List<Runnable> taskQueue = new LinkedList<>();
    private boolean running = true;

    // Developers had to MANUALLY implement all of this:
    // - Thread pooling
    // - Task queuing
    // - Synchronization
    // - Shutdown hooks
    // ... it was error-prone and boilerplate-heavy
}
```

