## 4. Runnable vs Callable vs Future

These three are the building blocks of task submission.

### 4.1 Runnable — Fire and Forget

`Runnable` is the oldest interface (since Java 1.0). It runs a task but **returns no result** and **cannot throw checked exceptions**.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class RunnableExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {

        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);

        // ✅ Lambda-style Runnable (Java 8+)
        Runnable task1 = () -> {
            System.out.println("Task 1 running on: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
        };

        // ✅ Anonymous class style
        Runnable task2 = new Runnable() {
            @Override
            public void run() {
                System.out.println("Task 2 running on: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
            }
        };

        executor.execute(task1);   // No return value
        executor.execute(task2);
        executor.submit(task1);    // submit() also works with Runnable — returns Future<?>

        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

**Output (order may vary):**
```
Task 1 running on: pool-1-thread-1
Task 2 running on: pool-1-thread-2
Task 1 running on: pool-1-thread-3
```

**Signature:**
```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface Runnable {
    void run();  // no return, no checked exception
}
```

---

### 4.2 Callable — Task with a Return Value

`Callable<V>` is the modern alternative to `Runnable`. It **returns a result** of type `V` and **can throw checked exceptions**.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class CallableExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);

        // ✅ Callable that returns an Integer
        Callable<Integer> sumTask = () -> {
            int sum = 0;
            for (int i = 1; i <= 100; i++) sum += i;
            System.out.println("Computed sum on: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
            return sum;
        };

        // ✅ Callable that might throw a checked exception
        Callable<String> fileReadTask = () -> {
            // Could throw IOException — perfectly legal in Callable
            Thread.sleep(1000); // simulate file reading
            return "File content read successfully";
        };

        // submit() returns a Future
        Future<Integer> sumFuture = executor.submit(sumTask);
        Future<String> fileFuture = executor.submit(fileReadTask);

        // Blocking call — waits until result is ready
        System.out.println("Sum = " + sumFuture.get());
        System.out.println("File = " + fileFuture.get());

        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```
Computed sum on: pool-1-thread-1
Sum = 5050
File = File content read successfully
```

**Signature:**
```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface Callable<V> {
    V call() throws Exception;  // returns V, can throw checked Exception
}
```

