### 4.3 Future — Handle on an Async Result

`Future<V>` is a reference to the result of an asynchronous computation. You get a `Future` when you call `executor.submit(callable)`.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class FutureExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);

        Callable<String> heavyTask = () -> {
            Thread.sleep(2000); // simulate heavy computation
            return "Heavy task done!";
        };

        // Submit and immediately get a Future (non-blocking)
        Future<String> future = executor.submit(heavyTask);

        System.out.println("Task submitted. Doing other work...");

        // ✅ isDone() — non-blocking check
        System.out.println("Is done? " + future.isDone()); // false — still running

        // ✅ get() — blocking, waits indefinitely
        String result = future.get();
        System.out.println("Result: " + result);

        // ✅ get(timeout, unit) — blocking with timeout
        // String result2 = future.get(3, TimeUnit.SECONDS); // throws TimeoutException if too slow

        // ✅ cancel(mayInterrupt)
        // future.cancel(true); // attempts to cancel the task

        System.out.println("Is done now? " + future.isDone()); // true

        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

**Key Future Methods:**

| Method | Description |
|---|---|
| `get()` | Blocks until result is ready; throws `ExecutionException` on task failure |
| `get(timeout, unit)` | Blocks up to timeout; throws `TimeoutException` |
| `isDone()` | Returns `true` if task completed (success, cancel, or exception) |
| `isCancelled()` | Returns `true` if cancelled before completion |
| `cancel(boolean)` | Tries to cancel; `true` = interrupt if running |

