## 5. ExecutorService — Core Interface

`ExecutorService` extends `Executor` and provides full lifecycle management.

### 5.1 submit() vs execute()

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class SubmitVsExecute {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);

        // execute() — void, no return, no exception propagation from task
        executor.execute(() -> System.out.println("execute: " + Thread.currentThread().getName()));

        // submit(Runnable) — returns Future<?> (result is null on success)
        Future<?> f1 = executor.submit(() -> System.out.println("submit runnable"));
        f1.get(); // blocks, then returns null; re-throws task exception as ExecutionException

        // submit(Callable<V>) — returns Future<V> with actual result
        Future<String> f2 = executor.submit(() -> "Hello from Callable");
        System.out.println(f2.get()); // "Hello from Callable"

        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

### 5.2 invokeAll() — Submit Multiple Tasks

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class InvokeAllExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);

        List<Callable<String>> tasks = Arrays.asList(
            () -> { Thread.sleep(300); return "Task A"; },
            () -> { Thread.sleep(100); return "Task B"; },
            () -> { Thread.sleep(200); return "Task C"; }
        );

        // invokeAll() blocks until ALL tasks finish
        List<Future<String>> futures = executor.invokeAll(tasks);

        for (Future<String> future : futures) {
            System.out.println("Result: " + future.get());
        }
        // Output (in original submission order):
        // Result: Task A
        // Result: Task B
        // Result: Task C

        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

### 5.3 invokeAny() — First Result Wins

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class InvokeAnyExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);

        List<Callable<String>> tasks = Arrays.asList(
            () -> { Thread.sleep(1000); return "Slow task"; },
            () -> { Thread.sleep(200);  return "Fast task"; },  // This wins!
            () -> { Thread.sleep(500);  return "Medium task"; }
        );

        // invokeAny() returns the FIRST successful result; cancels remaining
        String winner = executor.invokeAny(tasks);
        System.out.println("Winner: " + winner); // "Fast task"

        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

