## 6. Thread Pool Types

The `Executors` factory class provides convenience methods to create the most common pool configurations.

### 6.1 newFixedThreadPool(n)

Fixed number of threads. Ideal for CPU-bound tasks where you don't want more threads than cores.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class FixedThreadPoolExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {

        // ✅ Use when: you want predictable, bounded thread count
        int cpuCores = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors();
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(cpuCores);

        for (int i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
            final int id = i;
            executor.submit(() -> {
                System.out.println("Task " + id + " → " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
                try { Thread.sleep(500); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
            });
        }

        // Tasks 1-N run, rest wait in an unbounded LinkedBlockingQueue
        executor.shutdown();
        executor.awaitTermination(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        System.out.println("All tasks completed.");
    }
}
```

**Internals:** `LinkedBlockingQueue` (unbounded), fixed `corePoolSize = maxPoolSize`.

---

### 6.2 newCachedThreadPool()

Grows thread count on demand; reuses idle threads; removes threads idle for 60 seconds.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class CachedThreadPoolExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // ✅ Use when: many short-lived tasks; I/O-bound tasks
        // ⚠️ Risk: unbounded threads under high load
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();

        for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
            final int id = i;
            executor.submit(() ->
                System.out.println("Task " + id + " on " + Thread.currentThread().getName())
            );
        }

        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

**Internals:** `SynchronousQueue` (no buffering), `corePoolSize=0`, `maxPoolSize=Integer.MAX_VALUE`, 60s keepAlive.

---

### 6.3 newSingleThreadExecutor()

Exactly one thread. All tasks run sequentially in submission order.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class SingleThreadExecutorExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {

        // ✅ Use when: tasks must run sequentially; like a single-threaded event loop
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();

        executor.submit(() -> System.out.println("Step 1: Initialize"));
        executor.submit(() -> System.out.println("Step 2: Process"));
        executor.submit(() -> System.out.println("Step 3: Save"));
        executor.submit(() -> System.out.println("Step 4: Notify"));

        // Always runs in order: Step 1 → 2 → 3 → 4
        executor.shutdown();
        executor.awaitTermination(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }
}
```

---

### 6.4 newWorkStealingPool() (Java 8+)

Based on `ForkJoinPool`. Threads can "steal" tasks from other threads' queues to maximize CPU use.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class WorkStealingPoolExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {

        // ✅ Use when: irregular task sizes; recursive divide-and-conquer; parallelism
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newWorkStealingPool(); // uses availableProcessors()

        for (int i = 1; i <= 8; i++) {
            final int id = i;
            executor.submit(() -> {
                System.out.println("Task " + id + " on " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
            });
        }

        executor.awaitTermination(3, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }
}
```

---

### 6.5 Custom ThreadPoolExecutor (Full Control)

When the factory shortcuts don't fit, build your own:

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class CustomThreadPoolExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        ThreadPoolExecutor executor = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
            2,                           // corePoolSize: always-alive threads
            5,                           // maximumPoolSize: max threads under load
            30L,                         // keepAliveTime: idle time before shrinking
            TimeUnit.SECONDS,
            new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(10), // bounded task queue (capacity=10)
            Executors.defaultThreadFactory(),
            new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy() // rejection policy
        );

        // Rejection Policies:
        // AbortPolicy (default) — throws RejectedExecutionException
        // CallerRunsPolicy    — task runs on the calling thread
        // DiscardPolicy       — silently discards the task
        // DiscardOldestPolicy — discards the oldest queued task, retries

        for (int i = 1; i <= 20; i++) {
            final int id = i;
            try {
                executor.execute(() -> {
                    System.out.println("Task " + id + " on " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
                    try { Thread.sleep(200); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
                });
            } catch (RejectedExecutionException e) {
                System.out.println("Task " + id + " was rejected!");
            }
        }

        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

### 6.6 Thread Pool Types — Comparison

| Pool Type | Threads | Queue | Best For |
|---|---|---|---|
| `newFixedThreadPool(n)` | Fixed `n` | Unbounded `LinkedBlockingQueue` | CPU-bound, stable workloads |
| `newCachedThreadPool()` | 0 to ∞ | `SynchronousQueue` | Many short I/O tasks |
| `newSingleThreadExecutor()` | 1 | Unbounded `LinkedBlockingQueue` | Sequential task processing |
| `newWorkStealingPool()` | CPU cores | Work-steal deque | Fork/Join parallelism |
| `ThreadPoolExecutor` (custom) | configurable | configurable | Production fine-tuning |

