## 9. Shutdown & Lifecycle Management

Proper shutdown is critical to avoid thread leaks and resource exhaustion.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class ShutdownExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {

        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);

        // Submit some work
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            executor.submit(() -> {
                try { Thread.sleep(1000); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
                System.out.println("Done: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
            });
        }

        // ✅ shutdown() — graceful: accepts no new tasks, finishes existing ones
        executor.shutdown();

        // ✅ awaitTermination() — waits for graceful completion
        if (!executor.awaitTermination(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
            // ✅ shutdownNow() — forceful: sends interrupt to running threads, returns pending tasks
            List<Runnable> notStarted = executor.shutdownNow();
            System.out.println("Force shut down. Pending tasks: " + notStarted.size());

            // Wait a bit more
            if (!executor.awaitTermination(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {
                System.err.println("Executor did not terminate!");
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Executor terminated: " + executor.isTerminated());
    }
}
```

### Lifecycle States

```
NEW → RUNNING → SHUTDOWN → STOP → TIDYING → TERMINATED

shutdown()     → SHUTDOWN (no new tasks, finish existing)
shutdownNow()  → STOP     (interrupt running threads)
isShutdown()   → true after either shutdown call
isTerminated() → true only after all tasks have finished
```

