## 10. Best Practices

### 10.1 Always Name Your Threads (Debugging)

```java
ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4, runnable -> {
    Thread t = new Thread(runnable);
    t.setName("payment-processor-" + t.getId());
    t.setDaemon(false); // don't silently die with main thread
    return t;
});
```

### 10.2 Always Handle Exceptions

```java
// With execute() — exception is swallowed unless you add a UncaughtExceptionHandler
// With submit() — exception is stored in the Future; re-thrown on get()

ExecutorService exec = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);

Future<?> f = exec.submit(() -> {
    throw new RuntimeException("Something went wrong");
});

try {
    f.get(); // Throws ExecutionException wrapping the original exception
} catch (ExecutionException e) {
    System.out.println("Caught: " + e.getCause().getMessage());
}
```

### 10.3 Size Thread Pools Correctly

```java
int cpuCores = Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors();

// CPU-bound tasks (no blocking I/O)
int cpuBoundPoolSize = cpuCores;

// I/O-bound tasks (high blocking time)
// Rule of thumb: N * (1 + wait_time / compute_time)
int ioBoundPoolSize = cpuCores * 4; // example for 75% wait time
```

### 10.4 Use try-with-resources Pattern (Java 19+ ExecutorService implements AutoCloseable)

```java
// Java 19+ — ExecutorService is AutoCloseable
try (ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4)) {
    executor.submit(() -> System.out.println("Task done"));
} // automatically calls shutdown() and awaitTermination()
```

### 10.5 Prefer submit() over execute() for Tasks That May Throw

```java
// execute() — exceptions are lost unless UncaughtExceptionHandler is set
executor.execute(() -> riskyMethod()); // ❌ exception may disappear

// submit() — exception is captured in Future
Future<?> f = executor.submit(() -> riskyMethod()); // ✅
f.get(); // re-throws as ExecutionException
```

