## 11. Common Pitfalls

### 11.1 Not Shutting Down the Executor

```java
// ❌ WRONG — JVM will NOT exit! Non-daemon threads keep it alive.
ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);
executor.submit(() -> System.out.println("Task"));
// Missing executor.shutdown()!

// ✅ CORRECT
executor.shutdown();
```

### 11.2 Calling get() Without Timeout — Risk of Deadlock

```java
// ❌ DANGEROUS
String result = future.get(); // blocks forever if task hangs

// ✅ SAFE
String result = future.get(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS); // throws TimeoutException
```

### 11.3 Using newCachedThreadPool() for CPU-Bound Tasks

```java
// ❌ DANGEROUS for CPU-bound work — creates thousands of threads
ExecutorService bad = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();

// ✅ Bounded pool for CPU-bound
ExecutorService good = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors());
```

### 11.4 Ignoring Task Rejection

```java
// ❌ WRONG — silently loses tasks when queue is full
ThreadPoolExecutor exec = new ThreadPoolExecutor(2, 5, 30, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
    new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(10),
    new ThreadPoolExecutor.DiscardPolicy()); // tasks silently dropped!

// ✅ BETTER — log and handle
new ThreadPoolExecutor.RejectedExecutionHandler() {
    @Override
    public void rejectedExecution(Runnable r, ThreadPoolExecutor executor) {
        System.err.println("REJECTED: " + r.toString());
        // Optionally: persist to DB, put in retry queue, etc.
    }
}
```

